{"_type":"item","id":"019d5c00000000000000000000000001","type":"skill","status":"active","title":"Agent Supervision","description":"Guidelines for supervising and coordinating sub-agents in Minerva.","steps":"## Setup\n1. Use minerva_list_models (NO provider filter) to see ALL available providers and enum IDs.\n2. Choose the right provider for each worker. Pass provider= to spawn_worker. IMPORTANT: 'chatgpt' and 'openai' are DIFFERENT providers. If the user says 'ChatGPT', use provider='chatgpt'.\n3. For GPT/ChatGPT workers, ALWAYS pass skills= for static tool mode — they cannot do dynamic discovery. Example: skills=[\"Co-Browser Automation\"].\n4. For Gemini/Claude workers, skills= is optional but still saves rounds.\n\n## Spawning\n5. Spawn all workers FIRST, then monitor. Do not spawn one, wait, then spawn the next.\n6. Give each worker a COMPLETE task description. Include everything it needs to know — the worker cannot see your conversation or ask you questions.\n\n## Monitoring — HANDS OFF\n7. After spawning, do your own setup work (create spreadsheet, etc.) before checking workers. Workers need time to run.\n8. Use minerva_check_worker to monitor (cheap, ~100 tokens). When checking multiple workers, batch ALL check_worker calls into ONE parallel tool round.\n9. Check each worker AT MOST twice before it completes. Do NOT rapid-fire poll. If a worker is still running, do something else and check back later.\n10. NEVER call minerva_get_chat_history on a running worker. After status='completed', call minerva_get_chat_history once to get the metadata list, then hydrate only the specific messages you need with minerva_get_chat_messages.\n11. NEVER call task-specific tools yourself (cobrowser, file tools, etc.). Your job is to COORDINATE, not to DO the work. If a worker fails, send guidance or spawn a replacement — do not duplicate its work.\n\n## Communication\n12. NEVER send a message to a worker with status='completed'. Sending restarts it, wasting budget. Read its output and move on.\n13. Only use minerva_send_message if a worker is stuck (status='running' but rounds_used hasn't changed across checks). Keep messages short and actionable.\n\n## Finishing\n14. Once ALL workers have completed: read each worker's metadata list ONCE via minerva_get_chat_history, hydrate only the final assistant message or other specific indices you need via minerva_get_chat_messages, populate output (spreadsheet, etc.), and STOP.\n15. Do NOT hydrate all messages unless the user explicitly needs the full transcript. Completion notifications already include the worker's last response preview, which may be enough.\n16. Present results to the user exactly ONCE. Do not re-read transcripts, re-check workers, or regenerate your summary.\n\n## Spreadsheets\n17. If the user asks for a spreadsheet: minerva_tool_search('spreadsheet'), create with minerva_create_spreadsheet_editor, populate with minerva_update_spreadsheet_data.","preconditions":"DocketManager loaded, at least one project docket open.","outcome":"Work is decomposed and completed through coordinated sub-agents with minimal supervisor overhead.","tool_deps":["minerva_spawn_worker","minerva_list_workers","minerva_check_worker","minerva_approve_workers","minerva_send_message","minerva_get_chat_history","minerva_get_chat_messages","minerva_list_models","minerva_list_chats"],"tags":"agent-supervision,workflow","created_at":"2026-04-05T03:00:00","updated_at":"2026-04-08T05:00:00"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d5c00000000000000000000000002","type":"skill","status":"active","title":"Knowledge Management","description":"How to use docket hints, insights, and quality scoring to build persistent knowledge.","steps":"1. Before starting work on a component, check for existing knowledge: docket_hint_query(component=<name>).\n2. If hints exist, read them and apply. Bump useful hints with docket_quality(score=+3).\n3. During work, when you discover something non-obvious (a gotcha, working command, selector, pattern), save it immediately: docket_hint_set(component, key, value, tags).\n4. If you discover a mental model correction (you assumed X but Y is true), create an insight: docket_create(type=insight, assumed=X, corrected=Y).\n5. If knowledge caused errors or was outdated, downgrade it: docket_quality(score=-3, reason='outdated').\n6. Keep hint values short and actionable — one-liners, small JSON, exact commands.\n7. Use component names that match the system being worked on (e.g., 'godot', 'docket', 'cobrowser').","preconditions":"Docket tools available (minerva_tool_search 'docket hint' to activate).","outcome":"Knowledge accumulates across sessions. Future agents benefit from past discoveries without re-learning.","tool_deps":["minerva_docket_hint_set","minerva_docket_hint_get","minerva_docket_hint_query","minerva_docket_quality"],"tags":"tool-usage,knowledge,hints","created_at":"2026-04-05T03:00:00","updated_at":"2026-04-05T03:00:00"}
{"_type": "item", "id": "019d5c00000000000000000000000003", "type": "skill", "status": "active", "title": "Tool Usage Patterns", "description": "Efficient patterns for using Minerva's MCP tools.", "steps": "1. Tools are auto-activated when you call minerva_get_skill — check the response for activated_tools. Do NOT call minerva_tool_search for tools that were already auto-activated.\n2. Only use minerva_tool_search for tools NOT covered by a skill you loaded, or when a tool call fails with \"not loaded\".\n3. For spreadsheets: minerva_create_spreadsheet_editor to create, minerva_update_spreadsheet_data to populate (csv_content for bulk, cells for individual updates).\n4. For terminal work: minerva_terminal_create, then minerva_terminal_write with \\r for Enter (not \\n).\n5. For webviews: minerva_create_webview_panel with HTML.\n6. For notes: minerva_create_note for persistent context.\n7. Prefer targeted queries over broad fetches. If a result is truncated, narrow your query.\n8. Complete tasks in as few tool calls as possible while being thorough.\n9. Tools stay activated for the entire session — do NOT re-search for tools you already have.", "preconditions": "Minerva MCP tools connected.", "outcome": "Efficient tool usage with minimal wasted calls.", "tool_deps": [], "tags": "tool-usage,efficiency,patterns", "created_at": "2026-04-05T03:00:00", "updated_at": "2026-04-05T03:00:00"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d5c00000000000000000000000004","type":"skill","status":"active","title":"Docket Work Tracking","description":"How to use docket for tracking and managing work items.","steps":"1. Activate docket tools: minerva_tool_search('docket create') or minerva_tool_search('docket query').\n2. Create work items with clear titles and descriptions: docket_create(type=work_item, title=..., description=...).\n3. Use parent fields to build hierarchies: docket_create(parent=<parent_id>).\n4. Follow state machines — work_item: backlog → open → in_progress → done.\n5. Use docket_transition to move items through states. Include a note explaining why.\n6. Use docket_query to find items by type, status, tags, or text search.\n7. Use docket_comment to add discussion to items.\n8. Use docket_link to create relationships (blocks, caused_by, follow_up).\n9. For bugs: type=bug, states: new → triaged → active → resolved → verified → closed.\n10. For design changes: type=dcr, states: proposed → approved → designing → implementing → reviewing → shipped.","preconditions":"At least one project docket loaded. Activate tools via minerva_tool_search('docket').","outcome":"Work is tracked with full history, relationships, and state machine enforcement.","tool_deps":["minerva_docket_create","minerva_docket_query","minerva_docket_transition","minerva_docket_update","minerva_docket_comment","minerva_docket_get"],"tags":"tool-suite,docket,work-tracking","created_at":"2026-04-05T03:00:00","updated_at":"2026-04-05T03:00:00"}
{"_type": "item", "id": "019d5c00000000000000000000000005", "type": "skill", "status": "active", "title": "Co-Browser Automation", "description": "Efficient web browsing and data extraction with cobrowser tools. Core pattern: navigate, verify with page_identity, extract with page_section fields maps, commit results immediately.", "steps": "1. Create a browser tab with cobrowser_tab_new(). This returns a tab_id for all subsequent commands.\n2. Navigate directly to the target URL with cobrowser_navigate. For JS-heavy sites (Amazon, Google, SPAs), use delay=2 or delay=3 to allow page hydration. Do NOT navigate to a homepage first unless the task requires it.\n3. After EVERY navigation, call cobrowser_page_identity. Verify the page type, title, and URL match expectations. If wrong, try ONE recovery (direct navigate or targeted click). If still wrong, cobrowser_request_human.\n4. If cobrowser_navigate returned _injected_knowledge (site-specific selector hints), read them BEFORE building your first fields map. Use the injected selectors exactly as given — they were verified on this site. Do NOT guess alternative selectors when hints are provided.\n5. For pages with repeating items (search results, product cards, table rows), use cobrowser_page_section with a fields map on the PARENT CONTAINER — the element that WRAPS all the repeating items, NOT the item selector itself. To find the parent container: use page_structure or query_all to identify the repeated item pattern, then target its parent wrapper (common patterns: main, [role='main'], .search-results, .results-list). Do NOT use the individual item selector — that matches one item, not all of them. Example: page_section(selector='.results-list', fields={'title': 'h2', 'price': '.price', 'url': 'a@href'}). Append @attribute to extract attributes instead of text (e.g. 'a@href' for links, 'img@src' for images). This is the primary extraction tool — one call can return all items on a page.\n6. If fields extraction returns 0 items or missing data, do NOT retry with the same selectors and do NOT fall back to per-item scraping. Instead: (a) call page_section on ONE individual result element WITHOUT the fields parameter to see its raw HTML structure — read the text, links, and buttons output to identify the correct CSS selectors, (b) retry the fields extraction on the PARENT CONTAINER with corrected selectors. This is a 2-call recovery, not a per-item loop.\n7. If @attribute extraction (e.g. a@href, img@src) returns null in a fields map, do NOT fabricate URLs or data. Instead, call page_section WITHOUT fields on one element — the links and buttons output always includes hrefs. Use those to build correct selectors or extract data directly.\n8. Use cobrowser_query_all to discover elements, count matches, and get xpaths. It returns metadata (tag, class, text snippet, xpath) per element. Use it when you need to find container selectors, verify element counts, or get xpaths for subsequent targeted reads. Note: query_all text snippets often contain enough data (names, prices, ratings) to complete the task without per-item reads. Check the snippets before drilling into individual elements.\n9. NEVER fabricate, estimate, or fill in data that was not in the extraction results. If a field is null, partial, or missing, report it as-is or mark it unavailable. Do NOT guess prices, invent URLs, or pad incomplete values. Let the user decide what to do with gaps.\n10. Once you have structured results, commit them to the output immediately (spreadsheet, note, or response). Do NOT re-extract the same page unless a tool call visibly failed. Do NOT search tool call history for data you already have in context.\n11. For detail pages: only navigate to individual items if the listing page lacks data you need. After drilling in, use page_identity to verify, then a targeted page_section read.\n12. cobrowser_scroll: only when content is below the fold or the page uses infinite scroll.\n13. cobrowser_click / cobrowser_type: only for required interactions — filters, forms, pagination, login.\n14. cobrowser_page_structure: escalation tool only. Use when page_identity and page_section are insufficient to understand page layout.\n15. CSS selector gotcha: element IDs with hyphens, dots, or other special characters (common on Amazon, Google, and SPAs) are invalid as bare CSS ID selectors. Do NOT use #uuid-with-hyphens. Instead use attribute selectors like [data-cel-widget='search_result_2'] or [id='uuid-with-hyphens'], or use xpath. When query_all returns xpaths, prefer those over constructing CSS selectors from IDs.\n16. AVOID cobrowser_get_state (returns entire DOM, very expensive), cobrowser_screenshot (slow, rarely needed), cobrowser_tab_claim (multi-agent coordination only), cobrowser_native_* (OS-level dialogs only).\n17. If a tool call returns a POLICY BLOCK, read the error message and follow its instructions. A policy block means a required precondition was not met — it does NOT mean the tool is unavailable. Do NOT call minerva_tool_search for a tool that was policy-blocked.\n18. Budget: aim for 5-10 cobrowser calls per task. If you are past 15, stop and reconsider your approach.", "preconditions": "Cobrowser MCP server connected, Firefox extension active.", "outcome": "Efficient browsing with bulk extraction via fields maps, minimal per-item calls, and clear recovery patterns.", "tool_deps": ["cobrowser_tab_new", "cobrowser_navigate", "cobrowser_page_identity", "cobrowser_page_structure", "cobrowser_page_section", "cobrowser_query_all", "cobrowser_click", "cobrowser_type", "cobrowser_scroll", "cobrowser_request_human"], "tags": "tool-suite,cobrowser,web-automation", "created_at": "2026-04-05T08:00:00", "updated_at": "2026-04-12T15:30:00-07:00"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d5c00000000000000000000000006","type":"skill","status":"active","title":"File and Code Tools","description":"Guidelines for file manipulation and code editing tools.","steps":"1. Activate file tools: minerva_tool_search('file edit') or minerva_tool_search('glob grep').\n2. Use minerva_file_glob to find files by pattern before reading them.\n3. Use minerva_file_grep to search for specific content rather than reading entire files.\n4. Use minerva_file_read with offset/limit to read only the sections you need.\n5. When editing, use minerva_file_edit for targeted changes rather than rewriting entire files.\n6. Use minerva_bash for shell commands when file tools aren't sufficient.\n7. If a tool times out or fails, try a different approach rather than retrying the same thing.\n8. If results are truncated, use more specific queries to get the data you need.","preconditions":"CodeTools MCP server connected.","outcome":"Targeted file operations with minimal wasted reads.","tool_deps":["minerva_file_read","minerva_file_write","minerva_file_edit","minerva_file_glob","minerva_file_grep","minerva_bash","minerva_cwd"],"tags":"tool-suite,codetools,files","created_at":"2026-04-05T08:00:00","updated_at":"2026-04-05T08:00:00"}
{"_type": "item", "id": "019d6f20000000000000000000000001", "type": "skill", "status": "active", "title": "Spreadsheet Workflows", "description": "How to create, populate, verify, and format spreadsheets in Minerva. Use this when the task involves tables, CSVs, rankings, comparison grids, or spreadsheet output.", "steps": "1. If the user wants a spreadsheet, load this skill first instead of guessing tool names.\n2. Gather ALL data BEFORE creating the spreadsheet. Do not create the spreadsheet until you have every row ready to write. Extract first, create and populate second.\n3. Create and populate in one call: minerva_create_spreadsheet_editor with csv_content. This creates the sheet with data already in it.\n4. CSV formatting rules for csv_content:\n   a. Values with commas: wrap in double-quotes. Example: \"Anker Cable, 60W\" becomes a single cell.\n   b. Values with internal double-quotes: escape by doubling them (\"\"). Example: the text 6\" cable becomes \"6\"\" cable\" in CSV.\n   c. Values WITHOUT commas or quotes: do NOT wrap in quotes.\n   d. Prices: write as plain decimal numbers (9.99 not $9.99). Apply number_format=currency afterward.\n   e. Counts: plain integers (79900 not 79,900).\n   f. Only include columns you have data for. If a field is empty for every row, drop the column.\n5. For subsequent bulk data changes, use minerva_update_spreadsheet_data with csv_content. This clears the sheet and replaces all data.\n6. Default formatting -- apply after every create (3 parallel calls):\n   a. Header row: format_cells range=A1:{last_col}1 bg_color=#2C3E50 text_color=#FFFFFF bold=true\n   b. Column widths: set_column_width -- names ~300, prices ~70, counts ~80, short text ~120, URLs ~350\n   c. Currency columns: format_cells range={price_col}2:{price_col}{last_row} number_format=currency\n   No other formatting unless the user explicitly asks.\n7. Verify: call minerva_get_spreadsheet_data after writing. Check that column count matches expectations and no data spilled into extra columns.\n8. Generate the final answer from the verified spreadsheet contents, not from remembered tool outputs.\n9. Do NOT overwrite the spreadsheet with a different schema unless correcting a verified mistake.\n10. Do NOT embed emojis, unicode symbols, or decorative characters in cell data. Ratings should be numbers (4.7). Flags should be text (Premium-priced).\n11. Adding rows: minerva_add_spreadsheet_row appends after the last row containing data. Use minerva_insert_spreadsheet_row with a specific row number if you need precise placement.\n12. For formulas: minerva_set_cell_formula then minerva_recalculate. For repeated patterns: minerva_fill_down.\n13. Charts only when they help: minerva_add_spreadsheet_chart, then minerva_refresh_charts if data changed.\n14. Use targeted cell edits for small corrections and csv_content replacement for full rebuilds.", "preconditions": "Minerva spreadsheet tools available through MCP.", "outcome": "Spreadsheet tasks complete with the right tool surface loaded up front and the final answer grounded in the actual sheet.", "tool_deps": ["minerva_create_spreadsheet_editor", "minerva_update_spreadsheet_data", "minerva_get_spreadsheet_data", "minerva_set_cell_formula", "minerva_fill_down", "minerva_recalculate", "minerva_format_cells", "minerva_set_column_width", "minerva_set_row_height", "minerva_add_spreadsheet_chart", "minerva_refresh_charts", "minerva_add_spreadsheet_row", "minerva_add_spreadsheet_column", "minerva_insert_spreadsheet_row", "minerva_insert_spreadsheet_column", "minerva_delete_spreadsheet_row", "minerva_delete_spreadsheet_column", "minerva_list_charts", "minerva_get_chart_image", "minerva_update_chart"], "tags": "tool-suite,spreadsheet,tables,csv,analysis", "created_at": "2026-04-11T14:08:36-07:00", "updated_at": "2026-04-12T13:20:00-07:00"}
{"_type": "item", "id": "019d5c00000000000000000000000010", "type": "prompt", "status": "active", "title": "Agentic Base", "description": "Base system prompt for agentic mode. Bootstraps tool search and skill discovery.", "component": "system-prompt", "key": "agentic-base", "prompt_text": "You are an AI assistant with access to tools.\n\nPhase 1 — SET UP (do this first):\n1. Call minerva_list_skills to see available guides.\n2. If a skill description says it covers multiple areas (e.g. \"covers BOTH browsing and spreadsheet output — no need to load separate skills\"), load ONLY that skill. Do not load additional skills for areas it already covers.\n3. Call minerva_get_skill for each relevant skill. This auto-activates the tools you need — do NOT call minerva_tool_search for tools that a skill already activated.\n4. Only use minerva_tool_search for tools not covered by a skill.\n\nPhase 2 — EXECUTE:\nOnce your tools are ready, do the task. Do not search for more tools during execution.", "tags": "system-prompt,agentic", "created_at": "2026-04-05T08:00:00", "updated_at": "2026-04-13T03:30:00"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d6120d31e781f992f65468a21ec19","type":"kb","status":"active","title":"Amazon Search Anti-Dark-Pattern Guide","description":"Mandatory reading before any Amazon product search or purchase recommendation.","created_at":"2026-04-06T04:50:34","updated_at":"2026-04-08T02:30:00","tags":["amazon","cobrowser","dark-patterns","policy","shopping"],"component":"shopping","key":"amazon-anti-dark-patterns","summary":"Amazon listings use dark patterns that exploit LLMs. This guide gives agent-friendly countermeasures that work within a 25-round tool budget.","article":"## Amazon Dark Patterns That Affect LLMs\n\nSellers optimize product descriptions to manipulate LLM recommendations: persuasive copy, fake differentiation, inflated value claims. The default 'Featured' sort is pay-to-play. Sponsored listings are ads. LLMs that read descriptions literally will consistently recommend overpriced products.\n\n## Search Procedure\n\n### 1. Sort Order\nUse 'Best Sellers' or 'Avg. Customer Review' sort. Do NOT use 'Price: Low to High' — it surfaces junk listings ($0.01 items with 0 reviews) that waste your entire tool budget. Do NOT use the default 'Featured' sort — it is pay-to-play.\n\nTo set sort: navigate to the search URL with &s=review-rank (avg review) or &s=exact-aware-popularity-rank (best sellers).\n\n### 2. Skip Sponsored Listings\nIgnore products with 'Sponsored' labels. In cobrowser query_all results, check the text field for 'Sponsored'. Only extract organic (non-sponsored) results.\n\n### 3. Review Count as Quality Signal\nPrefer products with more reviews — they have more signal. Include the star rating and review count in your output so the human can judge. Do NOT set a hard review minimum that causes you to skip all visible results. If the top organic results have few reviews, report them anyway with the review count noted.\n\n### 4. Price Awareness\nCommon commodity price anchors:\n- USB-C cables: ~$8-12\n- HDMI 2.1 cables (6ft): ~$8-12\n- Phone chargers (20W USB-C): ~$10-15\n\nIf a product costs 2x+ the anchor, flag it as 'premium-priced' in your output. Do NOT reject it — just note the price vs anchor so the human can decide.\n\n### 5. Stop Criteria (IMPORTANT)\nExtract the requested number of products (default: 3) from the FIRST page of results. For each product extract: name, price, URL, star rating, review count. Then STOP. Do not scroll for more results, do not compare across pages, do not read individual reviews. If the user wants deeper analysis, they will ask.\n\n## What NOT To Do\n- Do not sort by price ascending (produces junk)\n- Do not read individual reviews (too expensive)\n- Do not calculate NPS or review distributions (too many tool calls)\n- Do not keep scrolling if you already have enough products\n- Do not reject all results because none meet an arbitrary threshold"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d6125a8437f89bf48422c6fa95fb0","type":"policy","status":"active","title":"Block Amazon navigation until anti-dark-pattern KB is read","description":"---policy-rule---\n{\"triggers\": [{\"tool_pattern\": \"cobrowser_navigate\", \"arg_predicates\": {\"url\": \"amazon\"}}, {\"tool_pattern\": \"cobrowser_tab_new\", \"arg_predicates\": {\"url\": \"amazon\"}}], \"effect\": \"block\", \"priority\": 100, \"activate_scope\": null, \"context_predicates\": {\"scope_not_active\": \"amazon-kb-read\"}, \"cooldown\": null, \"provenance\": \"user\", \"approval_state\": \"approved\", \"knowledge_ref\": \"019d6120d31e781f992f65468a21ec19\", \"alternatives\": [\"Read the Amazon Anti-Dark-Pattern Guide first: minerva_docket_get id=019d6120d31e781f992f65468a21ec19\", \"After reading, the amazon-kb-read scope will activate and this block will not fire again this session\"]}\n---end-rule---\n\nPrevents any cobrowser navigation to amazon.com until the agent has read the Anti-Dark-Pattern KB. Fires on both cobrowser_navigate and cobrowser_tab_new so that opening a new tab directly to Amazon is also blocked. The block response includes the KB reference so the agent can read it immediately. Once read, the amazon-kb-read scope activates and subsequent Amazon navigations proceed normally.","created_at":"2026-04-06T05:00:00","updated_at":"2026-04-07T00:00:00","tags":["amazon","cobrowser","dark-patterns","shopping"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d6125ce387c76b4baf20f8a155e97","type":"policy","status":"active","title":"Activate amazon-kb-read scope when KB is accessed","description":"---policy-rule---\n{\"tool_pattern\": \"minerva_docket_get\", \"arg_predicates\": {\"id\": \"019d6120d31e\"}, \"effect\": \"scope\", \"priority\": 50, \"activate_scope\": \"amazon-kb-read\", \"scope_max_actions\": 100, \"scope_ttl_ms\": 2147483647, \"context_predicates\": {}, \"cooldown\": null, \"provenance\": \"user\", \"approval_state\": \"approved\", \"knowledge_ref\": \"\", \"alternatives\": []}\n---end-rule---\n\nWhen the agent reads the Amazon Anti-Dark-Pattern KB (via docket_get), this activates the amazon-kb-read scope. This scope satisfies the block policy's scope_not_active predicate, allowing subsequent Amazon navigation to proceed. Scope lasts 100 tool calls or 10 minutes — sufficient for a full browser automation session.","created_at":"2026-04-06T05:00:00","updated_at":"2026-04-07T18:00:00","tags":["amazon","cobrowser","dark-patterns","shopping"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d5c00000000000000000000000020","type":"hint","status":"validated","title":"Amazon DOM selector patterns","description":"Verified selector patterns for Amazon product search pages. Discovered through trace analysis of cobrowser automation sessions.","value":"Product URLs: use a.a-link-normal@href (NOT h2 a@href — Amazon wraps h2 inside a, so h2 a matches nothing). Prices: use .a-offscreen inside .a-price for full price text (e.g. '$12.99'). Parent container for search results: .s-main-slot (wraps all result cards). CSS ID gotcha: Amazon uses UUIDs with hyphens as element IDs — use [id='...'] attribute selector, not #id. Sponsored label: check for 'Sponsored' text in query_all snippets to filter ads.","component":"cobrowser","key":"amazon-selectors","tags":["amazon","cobrowser","selectors","dom"],"target":"sonnet","created_at":"2026-04-12T12:00:00","updated_at":"2026-04-12T12:00:00"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d5c00000000000000000000000021","type":"policy","status":"active","title":"Inject Amazon selector hints on navigation","description":"---policy-rule---\n{\"triggers\": [{\"tool_pattern\": \"cobrowser_navigate\", \"arg_predicates\": {\"url\": \"amazon\"}}, {\"tool_pattern\": \"cobrowser_tab_new\", \"arg_predicates\": {\"url\": \"amazon\"}}], \"effect\": \"inject\", \"priority\": 90, \"activate_scope\": \"amazon-selectors-injected\", \"scope_max_actions\": 100, \"scope_ttl_ms\": 2147483647, \"context_predicates\": {\"scope_active\": \"amazon-kb-read\", \"scope_not_active\": \"amazon-selectors-injected\"}, \"cooldown\": null, \"provenance\": \"user\", \"knowledge_ref\": \"019d5c00000000000000000000000020\", \"alternatives\": []}\n---end-rule---\n\nFires AFTER the Amazon KB block has been satisfied (requires amazon-kb-read scope active). Injects Amazon-specific DOM selector patterns into the cobrowser_navigate result so the LLM has correct selectors from the first extraction attempt. Activates amazon-selectors-injected scope to prevent re-injection on subsequent navigations. Priority 90 (below the block at 100).","created_at":"2026-04-12T12:00:00","updated_at":"2026-04-12T12:00:00","tags":["amazon","cobrowser","inject","selectors"]}
{"_type": "item", "id": "019d5c00000000000000000000000030", "type": "skill", "status": "active", "title": "Amazon Shopping Comparison", "description": "End-to-end Amazon product comparison: search, extract, and build spreadsheet. Covers BOTH browsing and spreadsheet output — no need to load separate skills. Narrow tool set, low token footprint.", "steps": "1. Call cobrowser_tab_new to open a browser tab.\n2. Navigate to Amazon with cobrowser_navigate. Use &s=review-rank (Avg. Customer Review) or &s=exact-aware-popularity-rank (Best Sellers) sort. Use delay=3 for JS hydration.\n3. If navigate returns _injected_knowledge, read the selector hints BEFORE extraction. Use the injected selectors exactly as given.\n4. Verify with cobrowser_page_identity — confirm it says Amazon search results.\n5. Extract with cobrowser_page_section(selector='.s-main-slot', fields={...}). Use the injected selectors for field keys. If no injected hints, use: name='h2 span', price='.a-offscreen', rating='.a-icon-alt', url='a.a-link-normal@href'.\n6. Use cobrowser_query_all to count results and check text snippets for review counts and Sponsored labels. Skip items with 'Sponsored' text or /sspa/ URLs.\n7. If fields return null for a column, do ONE recovery: cobrowser_page_section on a single result WITHOUT fields to see raw HTML structure. Fix selectors and retry on .s-main-slot. Do NOT do per-item extraction loops.\n8. Once you have 6+ organic results with name/price/URL, STOP extracting. Commit to spreadsheet immediately.\n9. Build CSV: no commas in values (strip them), prices as plain numbers (9.99), counts as integers (79900). Only include columns with data.\n10. Create spreadsheet: minerva_create_spreadsheet_editor with csv_content.\n11. Format: minerva_format_cells header row (A1:{last_col}1, bg=#2C3E50, text=#FFFFFF, bold). Then minerva_set_column_width. Then minerva_format_cells currency on price column.\n12. Verify: minerva_get_spreadsheet_data to confirm data is correct.\n13. Flag products priced 2x+ above commodity anchor as Premium-priced in a Notes column.\n14. Budget: 5-7 cobrowser calls, 3-4 spreadsheet calls. If past 10 cobrowser calls, stop and reconsider.", "preconditions": "Cobrowser MCP server connected, Firefox extension active.", "outcome": "Spreadsheet with product comparison: name, price, rating, reviews, notes, URL for 6 organic results.", "tool_deps": ["cobrowser_tab_new", "cobrowser_navigate", "cobrowser_page_identity", "cobrowser_page_section", "cobrowser_query_all", "minerva_create_spreadsheet_editor", "minerva_get_spreadsheet_data", "minerva_format_cells", "minerva_set_column_width"], "tags": "amazon,shopping,comparison,spreadsheet,cobrowser", "created_at": "2026-04-13T03:15:00", "updated_at": "2026-04-13T04:00:00", "optimization": {"context_window": 8, "summary_mode": "deterministic", "tool_idle_turns": 0, "tool_budget": 3000}}
{"_type": "item", "id": "019d5c00000000000000000000000031", "type": "kb", "status": "active", "title": "Skill Authoring Guide", "description": "Reference for creating and tuning Minerva skills. Covers fields, optimization profiles, and best practices.", "component": "skills", "key": "skill-authoring-guide", "tags": ["skills", "authoring", "optimization", "reference"], "summary": "How to write effective Minerva skills: field reference, optimization knobs, narrow vs broad design, and default values.", "article": "## Skill Fields\n\n### Required\n- **title**: Short, descriptive name. Include the domain and action (e.g. \"Amazon Shopping Comparison\" not \"Shopping Skill\").\n\n### Core Optional (what makes a skill useful)\n- **steps**: Numbered, imperative instructions. Use exact tool names. End with a budget step (\"aim for N calls\"). Each step should be one clear action.\n- **tool_deps**: Array of tool names auto-activated on skill load. List ONLY the tools the skill actually uses — fewer tools = lower token footprint in the API request.\n- **description**: One-line summary shown in list_skills results. If the skill covers multiple areas (browsing + spreadsheet), say so explicitly: \"Covers BOTH X and Y — no need to load separate skills.\"\n- **preconditions**: What must be true before using the skill (e.g. \"Cobrowser MCP server connected\").\n- **outcome**: What success looks like when the skill completes.\n\n### Discovery\n- **tags**: Comma-separated. Used by list_skills(tags=[...]) for filtering. Use category tags like \"tool-suite\", \"cobrowser\", \"spreadsheet\", \"amazon\", \"shopping\".\n- **component**: Organizational grouping (e.g. \"cobrowser\", \"spreadsheet\", \"shopping\").\n\n### Optimization Profile\nThe **optimization** field is a JSON dict that tunes runtime behavior when the skill is loaded. All keys are optional — omitted keys use global defaults from preferences.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"context_window\": 8,\n  \"summary_mode\": \"deterministic\",\n  \"tool_idle_turns\": 0,\n  \"tool_budget\": 3000\n}\n```\n\n#### context_window (int)\nHow many recent tool results stay live (full content) in the LLM context. Older results are dehydrated to compact summaries.\n- **Default**: global setting from preferences (typically 5-8)\n- **8**: Good for 10-20 turn tasks. Covers a full extract-then-commit cycle.\n- **12-15**: For longer multi-phase tasks (browse site A, then site B, then compare).\n- **0 or omit**: Use global default.\n\n#### summary_mode (string)\nHow retired tool results are summarized.\n- **\"deterministic\"**: No LLM summary calls. Uses pre-built ToolSummary strings. Zero wall time cost, zero token cost. Best for short tasks (<20 turns) where the dehydrated context is mostly setup (skill loading, policy compliance).\n- **\"llm\"**: Calls the configured summary model. Better quality summaries but adds 20-40s wall time per batch. Best for long sessions where summary quality matters.\n- **Omit**: Use global default from preferences.\n\n#### tool_idle_turns (int)\nEvict tools unused for N turns, regardless of budget. Keeps the tool list lean during long sessions.\n- **0**: Disabled. Use when tool_deps is small (<15 tools) — no need to evict.\n- **7-10**: Good for broad skills with 20+ tools. Evicts tools from earlier phases (e.g. spreadsheet tools during cobrowser extraction).\n- **3**: Too aggressive — tools get evicted before the setup phase completes.\n\n#### tool_budget (int, tokens)\nMax tokens for tool schemas in the API request. Controls how many tools the LLM sees.\n- **3000**: Tight. Good for narrow skills with <12 tools (~1500-2000 tokens).\n- **6000**: Medium. Fits 25-30 tools.\n- **10000**: Generous. Fits 50+ tools. Use when loading multiple broad skills.\n- **Omit**: Use global default (10000).\n\n## Design Principles\n\n### Narrow vs Broad Skills\n- **Narrow composite skills** (e.g. \"Amazon Shopping Comparison\"): bundle tools from multiple domains (cobrowser + spreadsheet) for a specific workflow. 9-12 tools, tight budget, deterministic summary. Best for common repeatable tasks.\n- **Broad generic skills** (e.g. \"Co-Browser Automation\"): cover an entire tool suite with general-purpose guidance. 10-20 tools, larger budget. Best for ad-hoc work where the specific task isn't known in advance.\n- **Prefer narrow** when you know the workflow. The LLM loads one skill instead of two, sees fewer tools, follows tighter instructions, and uses fewer tokens.\n\n### Steps Writing Tips\n- Use exact tool names: \"cobrowser_page_section\" not \"page section tool\"\n- Include CSS selectors or patterns when known: \"selector='.s-main-slot'\"\n- Include recovery patterns: \"If X returns null, do Y. Do NOT do Z.\"\n- End with a budget: \"Budget: 5-7 cobrowser calls, 3-4 spreadsheet calls.\"\n- Reference injected knowledge: \"If navigate returns _injected_knowledge, read the hints BEFORE building your fields map.\"\n\n### tool_deps Sizing\n| tool_deps count | Est. tokens | Budget needed |\n|----------------|-------------|---------------|\n| 5-9            | 1000-1500   | 3000          |\n| 10-15          | 1500-2500   | 4000-6000     |\n| 16-25          | 2500-4500   | 6000-10000    |\n| 25+            | 4500+       | 10000+        |\n\n### Example: Narrow Skill\n```\ntitle: \"Amazon Shopping Comparison\"\ntool_deps: [cobrowser_tab_new, cobrowser_navigate, cobrowser_page_identity,\n            cobrowser_page_section, cobrowser_query_all,\n            minerva_create_spreadsheet_editor, minerva_get_spreadsheet_data,\n            minerva_format_cells, minerva_set_column_width]\noptimization: {context_window: 8, summary_mode: \"deterministic\",\n               tool_idle_turns: 0, tool_budget: 3000}\n```\n9 tools, ~1500 tokens, fits in 3000 budget with room for protected tools.", "created_at": "2026-04-13T04:30:00", "updated_at": "2026-04-13T04:30:00"}
