{"_type":"item","id":"019d8ad9047579b59256247bfc129822","type":"skill","status":"active","title":"Text Editor","description":"How to create, populate, read, save, rename, and close text/code editor tabs in Minerva. Use this when the task involves writing code, drafting documents, or any plain-text editor surface.","created_at":"2026-04-14T00:40:05-07:00","updated_at":"2026-04-14T00:40:05-07:00","tags":["editor","text","code","files"],"summary":"Activates Minerva's text-editor MCP tools and gives the canonical create → populate → save → verify flow.","preconditions":"Minerva text-editor MCP tools available. (Activated automatically when this skill is loaded — do NOT call minerva_tool_search for them.)","steps":"1. If the user wants to draft, edit, or view code/text in a Minerva tab, load this skill first instead of guessing tool names. The text-editor tools auto-activate.\n\n2. Decide up front whether the editor should be backed by a file:\n   a. Scratchpad / draft: omit file_path. The tab lives in-memory; minerva_save_editor will prompt for a path later.\n   b. Backed by an existing or new file: pass file_path on minerva_create_text_editor. Subsequent minerva_save_editor calls write to that path with no extra arguments.\n\n3. Create and populate in one call when possible: minerva_create_text_editor(name=..., content=..., file_path=...). Skip a second update_editor round when the initial content is already known.\n\n4. Use a clear, unique name for the tab — it is the handle for every subsequent call. Avoid generic names like \"Untitled\" or \"tab1\" that collide with other editors.\n\n5. To replace content wholesale, call minerva_update_editor(editor_name, content). This overwrites — there is no patch/diff API. For large files, assemble the full new content in your reasoning before the call.\n\n6. To inspect what is currently in the editor (after user edits, or to verify your write landed), call minerva_get_editor_content(editor_name). Treat this as the source of truth, not your last update_editor payload.\n\n7. Persist to disk with minerva_save_editor(editor_name). Pass file_path only when redirecting to a different location (\"save as\"); otherwise omit it to save back to the associated file.\n\n8. Use minerva_list_editors to discover open tabs when you do not already know the editor_name (e.g. continuing work across a session). It returns text, graphics, and spreadsheet editors — filter by type if needed.\n\n9. Rename with minerva_rename_editor(editor_name, new_name) when the document's purpose changes or to disambiguate from a colliding name. The editor_name handle changes immediately — use the new name for subsequent calls.\n\n10. Close with minerva_close_editor(editor_name). Pass force=true only when you have already saved or explicitly want to discard unsaved changes; otherwise let the user be prompted.\n\n11. Verify before reporting success: after a save, call minerva_get_editor_content (or read the file via minerva_file_read if you also want to confirm disk state) and check the content matches what you intended to write.\n\n12. Do NOT re-run minerva_tool_search for create_text_editor / update_editor / get_editor_content / save_editor / list_editors / rename_editor / close_editor — they are activated by this skill for the rest of the session.","outcome":"Text editor tasks complete with content correctly written, optionally persisted to disk, and verified from the live editor — not from remembered tool outputs.","tool_deps":["minerva_create_text_editor","minerva_update_editor","minerva_get_editor_content","minerva_save_editor","minerva_list_editors","minerva_rename_editor","minerva_close_editor"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d8ae63d8f78c830d93591926592cf","type":"skill","status":"active","title":"Skill Authoring","description":"How to author a Minerva skill at runtime — field contract, tool_deps sizing, and optimization knobs. Uses minerva_skill_create which handles insertion, active-status transition, and tool activation in one call.","created_at":"2026-04-14T00:40:05-07:00","updated_at":"2026-04-15T00:00:00-07:00","tags":["skills","authoring","meta"],"summary":"Runtime recipe for writing a skill that auto-activates its tools — one tool call, no file editing.","preconditions":"minerva_skill_create is available.","steps":"1. Clarify with the user: narrow composite (one workflow, 5-12 tools, tight budget) vs broad generic (an entire tool suite, 15-25 tools, larger budget). Prefer narrow when the workflow is known.\n\n2. Draft the skill's contract on paper first:\n   a. **title** — domain + action, unique in the project. Short — appears in the skill chooser.\n   b. **description** — one line that appears in skill_list results. Lead with when the skill should be used.\n   c. **preconditions** — what must already be true (MCP server connected, docket loaded, etc.).\n   d. **outcome** — what success looks like after the skill completes.\n   e. **steps** — numbered imperative actions. Use EXACT tool names, not paraphrases.\n   f. **tool_deps** — every MCP tool the skill might call. Auto-activation reads this.\n   g. **tags** — category filters for skill_list queries (e.g. \"editor\", \"spreadsheet\", \"cobrowser\").\n\n3. Steps-writing rules:\n   a. Number every step. One imperative action per step.\n   b. Use exact tool names: `minerva_create_text_editor`, not \"the text editor tool\".\n   c. Include known selectors / flags / literal args (\"use delay=3 for JS hydration\").\n   d. Include recovery patterns: \"If X returns null, do Y. Do NOT do Z.\"\n   e. End with a call budget: \"Aim for 5-7 tool calls; reconsider if past 10.\"\n   f. Last step: \"Do NOT re-run minerva_tool_search for <these tools> — they are activated by this skill.\"\n\n4. Size tool_deps deliberately. Every tool adds schema bytes to every request. Canonical bands:\n   a. 5-9 deps → ~1000-1500 tokens → tool_budget ≈ 3000.\n   b. 10-15 deps → ~1500-2500 tokens → tool_budget ≈ 4000-6000.\n   c. 16-25 deps → ~2500-4500 tokens → tool_budget ≈ 6000-10000.\n   d. Drop any tool the steps never name — no aspirational tools.\n\n5. Optional **optimization** profile fields (omit any key to fall back to global defaults):\n   - `context_window` (int) — live tool result window. 8 for 10-20 turn tasks, 12-15 for multi-phase.\n   - `summary_mode` — \"deterministic\" for short tasks (free, zero wall time); \"llm\" for long sessions where summary quality matters.\n   - `tool_idle_turns` (int) — evict tools unused for N turns. 0 when tool_deps < 15; 7-10 for broad skills.\n   - `tool_budget` (int, tokens) — see sizing table in step 4.\n\n6. Call `minerva_skill_create(title, description, steps, preconditions, outcome, tool_deps, tags, optimization)`. Status defaults to \"active\" and every entry in tool_deps is auto-activated.\n\n7. Verify with `minerva_get_skill(title=<name>)`. The response must include `\"activated_tools\": [...]` listing every entry from tool_deps. If some are missing, inspect `skipped_tools` in the skill_create response — those tool names were not found in the tool index. Fix the names and re-create.","outcome":"A new skill lives in the docket, status=active, and minerva_get_skill returns a populated activated_tools list matching tool_deps.","tool_deps":["minerva_skill_create","minerva_list_skills","minerva_get_skill","minerva_docket_skill_list"]}
